  <h1>What is the Double Ninth Festival?</h1>
  <div class="zh">重阳节</div>
  <p>A ninth-day autumn festival shaped by high places, chrysanthemums, seasonal customs, and a modern commitment to older people.</p>
  <p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin-top:10px">By <a href="/editorial-policy" style="color:inherit">yaopulife Editorial</a> · Published 2026-10-22 · Updated 2026-10-22</p>
</div></header>

<main class="wrap">
<p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin:18px 0"><a href="/">Home</a> · <a href="/festivals">Festivals</a> · Double Ninth Festival</p>
<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="answer">The quick answer</h2>
  <p class="lead" style="max-width:780px">The <b>Double Ninth Festival</b> (<span class="zh">重阳节</span> <i>Chóngyáng Jié</i>) falls on the <b>ninth day of the ninth lunar month</b>. Its name means “Double Yang”: traditional number symbolism associates nine with yang, so two nines make <i>chongyang</i>. Longstanding customs include climbing to a high place, viewing chrysanthemums, wearing sprigs of the plant <i>zhuyu</i> (<span class="zh">茱萸</span> <i>zhūyú</i>), and sharing flower cakes (<span class="zh">花糕</span> <i>huāgāo</i>) or chrysanthemum wine. In modern China, the date is also legally designated <b>Older Persons' Day</b>.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="name">What “Double Ninth” means</h2>
  <p>The name starts with the calendar. <i>Chóng</i> (<span class="zh">重</span>) means repeated or doubled, while <i>yáng</i> (<span class="zh">阳</span>) is the yang side of the yin-yang pairing. A Palace Museum account explains that nine was treated as a yang number; the ninth day of the ninth month therefore becomes “Double Yang.”</p>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Language note</span><b>“Ninth month” means the lunar month.</b> The festival does not fall on September 9 in the Gregorian calendar. Its Western-calendar date moves each year.</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="customs">Customs recorded together on this autumn date</h2>
  <p>Many Chongyang customs belong to an autumn day spent outdoors. The Palace Museum in Beijing records <b>climbing to high places</b>, wearing sprigs of <i>zhuyu</i> (<span class="zh">茱萸</span>), viewing chrysanthemums, drinking chrysanthemum wine, and eating flower cakes. Another Palace Museum study describes climbing and an outdoor feast as a durable core of the festival.</p>
  <p>The sources record a group of historical customs, not a required checklist for every person celebrating today.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="elders">What Chinese law says about Older Persons' Day</h2>
  <p>The festival's modern civic role is precise: Article 12 of China's <i>Law on the Protection of the Rights and Interests of Older Persons</i> designates the ninth day of the ninth lunar month as <b>Older Persons' Day</b> (<span class="zh">老年节</span> <i>Lǎonián Jié</i>). That legal designation reinforces a public emphasis on spending time with, caring for, and respecting older people.</p>
  <p>Calling Chongyang “China's Grandparents Day” can be a useful first analogy, but it is incomplete. Chongyang has a much older seasonal and ritual history, and Older Persons' Day includes older people generally—not only grandparents.</p>
</section>

